</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">September 2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The Agent Directory Service (ADS) is a distributed directory for the discovery
of AI agent capabilities, metadata, and provenance. It leverages
content-addressed storage, hierarchical taxonomies, and cryptographic signing to
enable efficient, verifiable, and multi-dimensional discovery across
heterogeneous Multi-Agent Systems (MAS). Built on the Open Agentic Schema
Framework (OASF), ADS decouples capability indexing from content location
through a two-level mapping realized over a Kademlia-based Distributed Hash
Table (DHT). It reuses mature OCI / ORAS infrastructure for artifact
distribution, integrates Sigstore for provenance, and supports schema-driven
extensibility for emerging agent modalities (LLM prompt agents, MCP servers,
A2A-enabled components). This paper formalizes the architectural model,
describes storage and discovery layers, explains security and performance
properties, and positions ADS within the broader landscape of emerging agent
registry and interoperability initiatives.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id4" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">K</em><span id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">eywords</span> Agent Directory Service  <math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Multi-Agent Systems  <math id="p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Capability Discovery</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) increasingly rely on composable AI agents that
encapsulate specialized capabilities such as natural language processing,
reasoning, computer vision, domain analytics, orchestration, or tool mediation.
As ecosystems diversify, discovery shifts from ad-hoc curation to infrastructure
that can answer questions such as: <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Which agents can perform regulated
clinical summarization with streaming JSON output?”</span> or <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Which
reasoning agents have signed provenance and acceptable latency profiles?”</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service (ADS) addresses this by providing: (i) a
structured, extensible schema layer (OASF) for expressing agent capabilities,
operational traits, and evaluation signals; (ii) a distributed storage and
indexing substrate reusing OCI registry semantics for durability and
interoperability; (iii) a taxonomy-driven discovery model enabling hierarchical
capability queries; and (iv) cryptographic integrity, provenance, and
verifiability via content addressing and Sigstore-based signing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Rather than prescribing a fixed agent execution architecture, ADS treats agent
definitions as immutable, content-addressed records enriched by extensions
(e.g., MCP server descriptors, evaluation metrics, prompt templates, feature
flags). This design maintains flexibility while supporting convergence toward
standard representational forms. Capability-based search is accelerated by a
two-level mapping: skills (and other taxonomic dimensions) to Content
Identifiers (CIDs), and CIDs to storage peers. This separation permits
horizontal scaling, adaptive replication, and low-latency intersection queries
across multiple classification axes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The reference specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and implementation
(<a href="https://github.com/agntcy/dir" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/agntcy/dir</a>) emphasize reuse of proven components: OCI
distribution for artifact transport, Kademlia DHT for routing
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and Sigstore for cryptographic provenance. ADS complements
parallel efforts in agent registries, identity, and interoperability (e.g., MCP
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, A2A <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and
decentralized naming discussions harkening back to Zooko’s Triangle
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>). In parallel, the NANDA suite of indexing and resolution
proposals  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>
explores DNS-inspired, adaptive resolver, enterprise governance, and comparative
survey dimensions of an Internet-scale agent naming fabric; ADS focuses instead
on capability-centric, content-addressed discovery and can interoperate with
such name resolution layers.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contributions.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C1</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Defines ADS architectural principles and layered model (schema,
storage, indexing, distribution, security).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C2</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Details the two-level discovery mapping and taxonomy strategy for
scalable capability search.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C3</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Explains integration of OCI / ORAS for artifact organization and
transport.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C4</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Formalizes security properties: integrity, provenance, trust
boundaries, and threat mitigation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C5</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Presents performance and replication strategies (on-demand retrieval,
proactive caching, strategic replication).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C6</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Situates ADS among related interoperability and registry initiatives.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Organization.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Design Goals ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> states design goals.
Section <a href="#S3" title="3 System Architecture Overview ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> overviews the architecture.
Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Storage Architecture ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> covers content-addressed storage and ORAS integration.
Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Discovery and Taxonomy Model ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> details discovery, taxonomies, and DHT mappings.
Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Security Model ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents the security model.
Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Performance and Replication ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> discusses optimizations.
Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Implementation Details ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> summarizes implementation and SDKs.
Section <a href="#S9" title="9 Related Work ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> reviews related work. Section <a href="#S10" title="10 Future Work ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>
outlines future directions and Section <a href="#S11" title="11 Conclusion ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> concludes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Design Goals</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The design of the Agent Directory Service (ADS) is driven by a set of
interrelated goals intended to balance openness, verifiability, and operational
pragmatism. We articulate these goals along with resulting architectural
constraints.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">G1 Extensible Schema Surface.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core representation <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MUST</em>
support forward-compatible evolution without invalidating previously published
records. OASF extensions (e.g., MCP server descriptors, evaluation metrics,
prompt bundles) <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SHOULD</em> compose additively and remain ignorable by nodes
that do not implement them. This motivates a loosely coupled extension registry
and strongly typed, versioned schema identifiers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">G2 Capability-Centric Discovery.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Discovery performance <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MUST</em>
scale sub-linearly with the total number of records for common capability
queries (skills, domains, features). This necessitates a separation between
capability indices and physical storage location (two-level mapping) and
encourages hierarchical taxonomies to bound combinatorial explosion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">G3 Verifiable Integrity and Provenance.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All records <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MUST</em> be
immutable and addressable by cryptographic digest (CID). Optional signing layers
(Sigstore) <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SHOULD</em> provide provenance without imposing key management
burden on casual publishers. Integrity verification <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MUST NOT</em> require
trusted intermediaries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">G4 Interoperability via Reuse.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SHOULD</em> reuse
existing, widely deployed distribution substrates (OCI registries, ORAS tooling)
to avoid bespoke artifact transport stacks and to leverage existing caching,
access control, and scanning ecosystems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">G5 Federated Operation.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Distinct organizations <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MUST</em> be able
to operate autonomous registries while participating in a shared discovery
plane. Federation <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SHOULD NOT</em> require global consensus on non-critical
metadata nor impose a single root of trust.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">G6 Multi-Dimensional Query Composition.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Queries combining at least
one skill plus optional domain and feature filters <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MUST</em> be executable
through efficient index intersections. Domain- or feature-only queries are
intentionally disallowed to preserve index locality and predictable complexity
bounds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">G7 Elastic Replication Strategies.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deployments <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SHOULD</em>
flexibly choose between on-demand retrieval, proactive caching, and strategic
replication depending on workload latency and availability requirements. The
architecture <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MUST</em> not hard-code a single replication policy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">G8 Security-in-Depth.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Threat mitigation (supply chain tampering,
spoofing, targeted DoS amplification) <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SHOULD</em> rely on layered primitives:
content addressing, transparent signatures, decentralized lookups, cache
isolation. No single compromised component <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SHOULD</em> yield undetectable data
forgery.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px9" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">G9 Operational Observability.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Index accuracy, replication lag,
signature verification failure rates, and cache effectiveness <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SHOULD</em> be
observable through exportable metrics to enable adaptive policy tuning (future
work elaborates evaluative telemetry extensions).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px10" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Resulting Constraints.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.2em;">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mutable metadata (e.g., dynamic performance measurements) are
represented as new versioned records or referrer objects, never in-place
edits.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All capability-bearing identifiers map first to CIDs; peer discovery
is a subsequent step (precluding location-dependent indexing shortcuts).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extension collision avoidance requires globally unique (namespace,
name, version) triples.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Discovery latency targets favor bounded taxonomy depth over
arbitrarily deep ontologies.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px10.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These goals collectively justify the layered decomposition detailed in
Sections <a href="#S3" title="3 System Architecture Overview ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4" title="4 Storage Architecture ‣ The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service:Architecture and Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>System Architecture Overview</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The AGNTCY Agent Directory Service (ADS) is architected as a layered, federated system for scalable agent capability discovery and artifact distribution. Its main components and flows are:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Layered Model.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ADS is organized into the following layers:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Schema Layer:</span> Defines agent records using the Open Agentic Schema Framework (OASF), supporting extensible, versioned metadata for skills, domains, features, and evaluation signals.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Indexing Layer:</span> Maintains capability-centric indices mapping skills, domains, and features to sets of agent record CIDs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Indices are published as immutable OCI artifacts and distributed via a DHT.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Storage Layer:</span> Stores agent records and referrers as OCI artifacts in one or more registries, backed by object storage (e.g., S3, GCS, Azure Blob). Records are content-addressed by cryptographic digest (SHA-256) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distribution Layer:</span> Uses mature OCI registry protocols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and ORAS tooling for artifact transport, caching, and replication. Federation allows organizations to operate autonomous registries while participating in a shared discovery plane.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Security Layer:</span> Provides cryptographic integrity, provenance, and verifiability via content addressing and Sigstore-based signing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Main Flows.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Publication:</span> Publishers create agent records (OASF), push them as OCI artifacts to a registry, and announce capability indices to the DHT. Optional referrers (signatures, evaluations) are attached using OCI referrer APIs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discovery:</span> Clients query the DHT for capability indices, resolve CIDs to registry endpoints, and retrieve agent records and associated referrers. Multi-dimensional queries intersect skill, domain, and feature indices for precise selection.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Replication and Federation:</span> Registries may replicate artifacts and indices across boundaries for performance, compliance, or availability. Federation is policy-driven and does not require global mirroring.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verification:</span> Clients verify record integrity using digest addressing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and, if present, validate signatures and provenance using Sigstore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This architecture enables scalable, secure, and extensible agent discovery and artifact distribution, supporting federated operation, multi-dimensional queries, and future schema evolution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Storage Architecture</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The storage subsystem provides durable, verifiable retention of agent records
and related referrers while enabling elastic distribution strategies. It centers
on cryptographic Content Identifiers (CIDs) and leverages OCI-compatible
registries for transport and persistence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Time Sequence: Record Publication and Retrieval</h3>

<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="544.34" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 384.77 544.34" width="384.77"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,544.34) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(37.79,0) translate(0,533.58)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -33.18 -4.8)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.17em;--ltx-fo-height:0.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:11.5pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="66.36"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Publisher</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 77.58 -3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.71em;--ltx-fo-height:0.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.17em;font-size:11.5pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="59.02"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.F1.pic1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Registry</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 196.31 -4.75)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.25em;--ltx-fo-height:0.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:11.5pt;" height="9.49" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="35.73"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.F1.pic1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DHT</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-c